/* book Worked example: Q: Design a cast-iron protected flange coupling for a steel shaft transmitting 15 kW at 200 rpm. Allowable stresses: shaft, key and bolts 40 MPa in shear and 80 MPa in crushing; cast iron 15 MPa in shear. Use four bolts and the usual proportions.
   1. Torque: T = 60 × 15 000/(2π × 200) = 716.2 N·m = 716 200 N·mm.
   2. Shaft: d³ = 16T/(πτ) = 16 × 716 200/(π × 40) = 91 190 mm³, d = 45.0 mm. Adopt d = 50 mm (τ = 29.2 MPa).
   3. Proportions: hub diameter 100 mm, hub length 75 mm, D1 = 150 mm, outer diameter 200 mm, flange t = 25 mm, rim 12.5 mm.
   4. Hub: τ = 16 × 716 200 × 100/(π(100⁴ − 50⁴)) = 3.89 MPa ≤ 15 MPa.
   5. Key 14 × 9 for a 50 mm shaft, force F = 2T/d = 28 648 N. Over 75 mm: τ = 28 648/(14 × 75) = 27.3 MPa (safe), σc = 28 648/(4.5 × 75) = 84.9 MPa, above 80 MPa. Make the hub and key 80 mm long: σc = 79.6 MPa.
   6. Flange shear at the hub: τ = 2T/(πD²t) = 2 × 716 200/(π × 100² × 25) = 1.82 MPa ≤ 15 MPa.
   7. Bolts: F = 2T/(nD1) = 2 × 716 200/(4 × 150) = 2387 N; db = √(4F/(πτ)) = √(4 × 2387/(π × 40)) = 8.72 mm. Adopt M10: τ = 2387/78.5 = 30.4 MPa, σc = 2387/(10 × 25) = 9.5 MPa.
   Answer: Shaft 50 mm; hub 100 mm × 80 mm; key 14 × 9 × 80 mm; four M10 fitted bolts on a 150 mm circle; flange 25 mm thick, outer diameter 200 mm, rim 12.5 mm. */
