/* book Worked example 2: Q: A bushed-pin coupling for a 30 kW, 720 rpm drive has six pins on a 160 mm pitch circle. Each pin is 14 mm in diameter and carries a rubber bush of 30 mm outer diameter; the pin load acts at the middle of the bush, 20 mm from the flange face. The bush pressure must not exceed 1 N/mm². Find the bush length and the stresses in the pin.
   1. Torque: T = 60 × 30 000/(2π × 720) = 397.9 N·m = 397 900 N·mm.
   2. Force per pin: F = 2T/(nD1) = 2 × 397 900/(6 × 160) = 829 N.
   3. Bush length: lb = F/(pDb) = 829/(1 × 30) = 27.6 mm; adopt 30 mm.
   4. Pin shear: τ = 829/(π × 14²/4) = 5.4 MPa.
   5. Pin bending: M = 829 × 20 = 16 580 N·mm; σb = 32M/(πd³) = 32 × 16 580/(π × 14³) = 61.5 MPa.
   6. Combined: τmax = √((61.5/2)² + 5.4²) = 31.2 MPa, well within a steel pin’s limit.
   Answer: Bush length 30 mm. The pin is stressed mainly in bending (61.5 MPa), not shear (5.4 MPa), which is why the bending check matters. */
